STORIES_LOAD("2sm",{"s2a":{"passage":[[1,1,4],[1,17,27]],"intro":[{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"A young Amalekite arrived with Saul's crown and armband. He bragged, \"I killed Saul myself!\" He thought David would reward him. But David said, \"Why weren't you afraid to strike the Lord's anointed king?\" and had him punished. The Bible tells us Saul had actually fallen on his own sword: the man was lying to win a reward."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"A messenger ran to David with torn clothes: \"Israel has lost. Saul and Jonathan are dead!\""},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"Many people would have celebrated. Saul had hunted David for years. Now the throne was open! But David tore his clothes and wept. He and his men fasted until evening."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"How the mighty have fallen! Saul and Jonathan, loved and lovely, in life and in death they were together. They were swifter than eagles, stronger than lions!"},{"sc":"ruinsfire","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"I grieve for you, my brother Jonathan. You were very dear to me."}],"lesson":{"title":"Honour even those who hurt you","text":"David didn't rejoice that his enemy had fallen. He honoured Saul publicly and grieved deeply for his friend Jonathan. Leaders refuse to celebrate other people's downfall, and they honour what was good in them."},"order":["A messenger brings the news","David hears Saul and Jonathan are dead","David tears his clothes and weeps","David and his men fast","David writes a song of mourning"],"quiz":[{"q":"How did David react to Saul's death?","o":["He wept and mourned","He celebrated","He laughed","He didn't care"],"why":"2 Samuel 1:11-12."},{"q":"What did David say Saul and Jonathan were swifter than?","o":["Eagles","Horses","The wind","Lions"],"why":"2 Samuel 1:23."},{"q":"Who did David call 'my brother'?","o":["Jonathan","Goliath","Joab","Samuel"],"why":"2 Samuel 1:26."}],"words":["MIGHTY","FALLEN","MOURN","EAGLES","LIONS","JONATHAN"]},"s2a2":{"passage":[[2,12,28]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"After Saul's death, the tribe of Judah crowned David king at Hebron. But Saul's commander, Abner, made Saul's son Ish-bosheth king over the rest of Israel. Now there were two kings, and two armies."},{"sc":"pool","n":"Abner, leader of Saul's army, and Joab, leader of David's army, met at the pool of Gibeon, one army on each side."},{"sc":"pool","w":"babner","e":"smirk","t":"Let some of the young men fight it out in front of us!"},{"sc":"pool","n":"Twelve men from each side stepped forward. They all grabbed each other at the same moment, and all twenty-four fell together. Then a fierce battle broke out, and Abner's army was beaten."},{"sc":"pool","n":"Joab's youngest brother, Asahel, could run as fast as a wild gazelle. He chased Abner and wouldn't turn aside to the left or the right."},{"sc":"pool","w":"babner","e":"worried","t":"Turn aside, Asahel! Chase someone else! Why should I strike you down? How could I face your brother Joab?"},{"sc":"pool","n":"But Asahel wouldn't stop. Abner struck back with the butt of his spear, and Asahel fell and died. Later Abner called out, \"Must the sword keep killing for ever? Don't you see this will end in bitterness?\" Joab blew the trumpet, and the fighting stopped."}],"lesson":{"title":"Know when to stop","text":"Asahel had amazing speed, but he wouldn't listen to warnings. The 'contest' turned into a deadly battle between brothers. And Abner asked a wise question: must the fighting go on for ever? Leaders know when to stop a fight before it ends in bitterness."},"order":["The two armies meet at the pool of Gibeon","Twelve men from each side fight","A fierce battle breaks out","Asahel chases Abner","Abner warns him to stop","Joab blows the trumpet to end the fighting"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where did the armies meet?","o":["The pool of Gibeon","Jericho","The Jordan","Mount Gilboa"],"why":"2 Samuel 2:13."},{"q":"How fast was Asahel?","o":["As fast as a wild gazelle","As fast as a horse","As slow as a turtle","As fast as an eagle"],"why":"2 Samuel 2:18."},{"q":"What wise question did Abner ask?","o":["\"Must the sword keep killing for ever?\"","\"Who is the king?\"","\"Where is Saul?\"","\"Who is fastest?\""],"why":"2 Samuel 2:26."}],"words":["GIBEON","POOL","ASAHEL","GAZELLE","ABNER","TRUMPET"]},"s2b":{"passage":[[2,1,4],[3,1,1],[3,22,39]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"David asked God, \"Where should I go?\" God said, \"Hebron.\" There, the men of Judah anointed David as their king."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"But Saul's commander, Abner, made Saul's son Ish-bosheth king over the rest of Israel. There was a long war between the house of Saul and the house of David."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"Eventually Abner came to make peace with David, and promised to bring all Israel to him. David welcomed him with a feast and sent him away in peace."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"But David's commander, Joab, hated Abner, because Abner had killed Joab's brother in a battle. Joab secretly called Abner back, took him aside at the city gate, and killed him in revenge."},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"I and my kingdom are innocent of this! A great man has fallen today. These sons of Zeruiah are too hard for me."}],"lesson":{"title":"Revenge breaks peace","text":"David was building peace. Joab's personal revenge nearly destroyed it. David publicly mourned Abner so everyone would know he hadn't ordered it. Leaders refuse revenge, and they deal openly with wrongs done in their name."},"order":["David is anointed king in Hebron","Abner makes Ish-bosheth king","A long war follows","Abner comes to make peace","Joab kills Abner in revenge","David mourns Abner publicly"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where was David first made king?","o":["Hebron","Jerusalem","Bethlehem","Gath"],"why":"2 Samuel 2:4."},{"q":"Why did Joab kill Abner?","o":["Revenge for his brother","David ordered it","Abner attacked David","By accident"],"why":"2 Samuel 3:27."},{"q":"How did David respond?","o":["He mourned Abner publicly","He rewarded Joab","He celebrated","He said nothing"],"why":"2 Samuel 3:31-32."}],"words":["HEBRON","ABNER","JOAB","REVENGE","PEACE","KING"]},"s2b2":{"passage":[[3,13,16],[4,1,12]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"As peace talks began, David demanded his first wife back: Michal, Saul's daughter. Saul had given her to another man, Paltiel."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"Paltiel walked behind her, weeping, all the way to the town of Bahurim, until Abner told him, \"Go home.\" A sad, hidden story inside the big one."},{"sc":"throne","n":"After Abner died, Saul's son Ish-bosheth lost all his courage. Two of his own captains, Rechab and Baanah, sneaked into his house in the hot afternoon while he napped, and killed him."},{"sc":"throne","n":"They rushed to David with proof, expecting a big reward: \"Here's your enemy! The Lord has paid back Saul!\""},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"frown","t":"When a man brought me news of Saul's death, thinking it was good news, I punished him. How much more, when wicked men kill an innocent man in his own bed!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"David had the murderers punished, and gave Ish-bosheth an honourable burial."}],"lesson":{"title":"Never reward wrongdoing","text":"The murderers thought David would be pleased. But David refused to gain from someone else's evil, even when it helped him. Leaders never reward people for doing wrong on their behalf."},"order":["David asks for Michal back","Paltiel follows her, weeping","Abner dies","Two captains kill Ish-bosheth","They bring the news to David","David punishes them"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who followed Michal, weeping?","o":["Paltiel","Saul","Abner","Jonathan"],"why":"2 Samuel 3:16."},{"q":"When did the murderers attack Ish-bosheth?","o":["In the hot afternoon while he napped","At midnight","At dawn","During a feast"],"why":"2 Samuel 4:5."},{"q":"How did David react to the murderers?","o":["He punished them","He rewarded them","He thanked them","He ignored them"],"why":"2 Samuel 4:12."}],"words":["MICHAL","PALTIEL","ISHBOSHETH","MURDER","REWARD","JUSTICE"]},"s2c":{"passage":[[5,1,12]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"Finally, all the tribes of Israel came to David at Hebron: \"You were the one who led us in battle. You will shepherd God's people.\" He was thirty years old."},{"sc":"jerusalem","n":"David wanted a capital city. He chose the fortress of Jerusalem, on a hill, held by the Jebusites. They mocked him: \"You'll never get in here! Even the blind and lame could keep you out!\""},{"sc":"jerusalem","n":"But David's men found a way in through the water shaft, a tunnel bringing water into the city. They captured the fortress!"},{"sc":"jerusalem","n":"David called it the City of David. He became greater and greater, because the Lord God of armies was with him. Even the king of Tyre sent cedar wood and builders to make him a palace."}],"lesson":{"title":"Find the way in","text":"The Jebusites thought their walls made them unbeatable. David's team found a creative route through the water tunnel. Leaders don't give up at the first wall; they look for another way, and they trust God to open it."},"order":["All the tribes come to David","David becomes king of all Israel","The Jebusites mock David","David's men go through the water shaft","They capture Jerusalem","It's called the City of David"],"quiz":[{"q":"How old was David when he became king?","o":["Thirty","Twelve","Fifty","Seventy"],"why":"2 Samuel 5:4."},{"q":"How did David's men get into Jerusalem?","o":["Through the water shaft","Over the wall with ladders","Through the gate","By boat"],"why":"2 Samuel 5:8."},{"q":"What was Jerusalem called after?","o":["The City of David","Babel","Hebron","Zion Town"],"why":"2 Samuel 5:9."}],"words":["JERUSALEM","FORTRESS","TUNNEL","WATER","ZION","CAPITAL"]},"s2c2":{"passage":[[5,17,25]],"intro":[{"sc":"treetops","n":"When the Philistines heard David was king, they came to attack. David asked God, \"Shall I go up?\" God said, \"Go!\" David won, and said, \"The Lord broke through my enemies like water bursting through a dam!\""},{"sc":"treetops","n":"The Philistines came back again. David asked God again, instead of assuming he knew the answer."},{"sc":"treetops","n":"This time God said, \"Don't attack from the front. Circle around behind them, near the balsam trees.\""},{"sc":"treetops","n":"\"When you hear the sound of marching in the tops of the balsam trees, move quickly! It means the Lord has gone out ahead of you.\""},{"sc":"treetops","n":"David listened, waited... heard the rustling in the treetops... and charged. Total victory!"}],"lesson":{"title":"Ask God every time","text":"David won the first battle, but he didn't assume the second would be the same. He asked again, and God gave a completely different plan. Leaders don't run on yesterday's instructions. They keep listening."},"order":["The Philistines attack","David asks God and wins","The Philistines attack again","David asks God again","God gives a different plan","David hears marching in the treetops"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did David hear in the tops of the balsam trees?","o":["The sound of marching","Birds singing","Thunder","Wind only"],"why":"2 Samuel 5:24."},{"q":"What did David do before the second battle?","o":["Asked God again","Used the same plan","Ran away","Asked Joab"],"why":"2 Samuel 5:23."},{"q":"What did David say God did to his enemies the first time?","o":["Broke through like bursting water","Froze them","Blinded them","Made them sleep"],"why":"2 Samuel 5:20."}],"words":["TREETOPS","MARCHING","BALSAM","LISTEN","WAIT","VICTORY"]},"s2d":{"passage":[[6,1,23]],"intro":[{"sc":"ark2","n":"David wanted to bring the ark of God to Jerusalem. They put it on a brand new ox cart, and everyone celebrated with music."},{"sc":"ark2","n":"But the oxen stumbled. A man named Uzzah reached out and grabbed the ark to steady it, and God struck him down. God had said the ark must be carried on poles by the priests, never touched or put on a cart."},{"sc":"ark2","n":"David was angry and afraid. He left the ark at the house of a man named Obed-edom for three months, and God blessed that family richly."},{"sc":"ark2","n":"This time they carried it God's way. Every six steps, David offered a sacrifice. And David danced before the Lord with all his might, wearing a simple linen robe!"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bmichal","e":"frown","t":"How glorious the king looked today, dancing in front of the servant girls like a fool!"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"stern","t":"I was dancing before the Lord! I'll become even more undignified than this to celebrate Him!"}],"lesson":{"title":"God's way, and wholehearted worship","text":"The first attempt failed because they did it their own way, copying how the Philistines moved the ark. The second time, they followed God's instructions, and David worshipped with everything he had, not caring what people thought. Leaders do things God's way, and they worship without embarrassment."},"order":["The ark is put on a new cart","Uzzah touches the ark","The ark stays with Obed-edom","The ark is carried God's way","David dances with all his might","Michal mocks David"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Uzzah do wrong?","o":["Touched the ark","Stole from it","Ran away","Dropped it"],"why":"2 Samuel 6:6."},{"q":"How did David worship as the ark came in?","o":["He danced with all his might","He sat quietly","He hid","He gave a speech"],"why":"2 Samuel 6:14."},{"q":"Who mocked David for dancing?","o":["Michal","Joab","Nathan","Abigail"],"why":"2 Samuel 6:20."}],"words":["ARK","DANCE","UZZAH","WORSHIP","LINEN","MUSIC"]},"s2e":{"passage":[[7,1,17],[7,18,22]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"Look, Nathan. I live in a beautiful palace of cedar, but the ark of God is in a tent! I want to build God a house."},{"sc":"throne","n":"That night, God spoke to the prophet Nathan: \"Will David build Me a house? No. I will build HIM a house: a family line, a kingdom that lasts for ever.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"\"Your son will build a temple for My name. And your throne will be established for ever.\" (Christians believe this promise points to Jesus, David's descendant, whose kingdom never ends.)"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"big","t":"Who am I, Lord God, and what is my family, that You have brought me this far? How great You are! There is no one like You!"}],"lesson":{"title":"God outgives us","text":"David wanted to do something great for God. God answered by doing something far greater for David. When we try to give to God, we find He always gives more. Leaders respond to God's goodness with humble wonder: 'Who am I?'"},"order":["David lives in a cedar palace","David wants to build God a house","God speaks to Nathan at night","God promises David a lasting kingdom","David's son will build the temple","David prays, \"Who am I?\""],"quiz":[{"q":"What did David want to build?","o":["A house (temple) for God","A bigger palace","A wall","A tower"],"why":"2 Samuel 7:2."},{"q":"What did God promise to build for David?","o":["A 'house': a family line and kingdom for ever","A palace","An army","A city"],"why":"2 Samuel 7:11-16."},{"q":"Who would build the temple?","o":["David's son","David","Nathan","Joab"],"why":"2 Samuel 7:13."}],"words":["HOUSE","PROMISE","FOREVER","NATHAN","THRONE","TEMPLE"]},"s2e2":{"passage":[[8,1,6],[8,13,18]],"intro":[{"sc":"battlehill","n":"God gave David victory after victory: over the Philistines, Moab, Zobah, Damascus and Edom. His kingdom grew from the river Euphrates to the border of Egypt."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Kings sent him gifts of gold, silver and bronze. David didn't keep them for himself. He dedicated them all to the Lord."},{"sc":"throne","n":"And the Bible gives David's reign one of its highest compliments: \"David reigned over all Israel, and did what was just and right for all his people.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"He organised a team: Joab over the army, Jehoshaphat as recorder, Zadok and Ahimelech as priests, Seraiah as secretary, and Benaiah over the bodyguards."}],"lesson":{"title":"Justice for all the people","text":"Success didn't make David selfish (yet). He gave the treasures to God, did what was just for ALL his people, and built a strong team. Leaders use success to serve others, and they share responsibility with good people."},"order":["David defeats the Philistines","His kingdom grows","Kings send gold and silver","David gives the treasures to God","David rules with justice","David organises his team"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did David do with the gold and silver gifts?","o":["Dedicated them to the Lord","Kept them","Spent them on parties","Buried them"],"why":"2 Samuel 8:11."},{"q":"How did David reign, according to the Bible?","o":["He did what was just and right for all his people","Cruelly","Lazily","Only for the rich"],"why":"2 Samuel 8:15."},{"q":"Who was over David's bodyguards?","o":["Benaiah","Joab","Zadok","Nathan"],"why":"2 Samuel 8:18."}],"words":["JUSTICE","RIGHT","VICTORY","GOLD","TEAM","KINGDOM"]},"s2f":{"passage":[[4,4,4],[9,1,13]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Years earlier, when news came that Saul and Jonathan had died, a nurse picked up Jonathan's five-year-old son, Mephibosheth, and ran. In her rush, she dropped him, and he became lame in both feet."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"Is there anyone left from Saul's family, so I can show them kindness for Jonathan's sake?"},{"sc":"throne","n":"They found Mephibosheth, living far away. He came and bowed low, terrified. In those days, new kings often wiped out the old king's family."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bmephib","e":"worried","t":"Who am I, that you should notice a dead dog like me?"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"Don't be afraid. I will give you back all your grandfather Saul's land. And you will always eat at my table, like one of my sons."}],"lesson":{"title":"Kindness to the forgotten","text":"David kept his promise to Jonathan years after his friend died. He went looking for someone who was hidden, disabled and afraid, and gave him honour, land and a place at his table. Leaders go looking for people to bless, especially the overlooked."},"order":["Jonathan's son is dropped and becomes lame","David asks about Saul's family","They find Mephibosheth","Mephibosheth is afraid","David gives him back his land","He eats at the king's table"],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose son was Mephibosheth?","o":["Jonathan's","Saul's","David's","Joab's"],"why":"2 Samuel 9:6."},{"q":"How did he become lame?","o":["His nurse dropped him as she ran","In a battle","From birth","A lion attacked"],"why":"2 Samuel 4:4."},{"q":"What did David give him?","o":["His family land and a seat at the king's table","Gold","An army","Nothing"],"why":"2 Samuel 9:7."}],"words":["KINDNESS","TABLE","JONATHAN","LAME","PROMISE","HONOUR"]},"s2f2":{"passage":[[10,1,14]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"The king of the Ammonites died, and his son Hanun became king. David said, \"His father was kind to me. I'll be kind to him,\" and sent ambassadors to comfort him."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bpeople","e":"frown","t":"My lord Hanun, do you really think David sent these men to comfort you? They're spies! They've come to look around the city!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"Hanun believed his suspicious advisers. He seized David's men, shaved off HALF of each man's beard, and cut their robes off at the hips, then sent them home!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"In those days, a beard was a man's honour. The ambassadors were so ashamed. David sent a message: \"Stay in Jericho until your beards grow back, then come home.\""},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"Hanun realised David was angry, and hired a huge army of Syrians. Joab said to his brother Abishai, \"Be strong! Let's fight bravely for our people. And may the Lord do what is good in His eyes.\" They won."}],"lesson":{"title":"Don't let suspicion destroy kindness","text":"David offered kindness. Hanun listened to suspicious advisers, insulted the messengers, and started a war he couldn't win. And David protected his embarrassed men. Leaders check the facts before assuming the worst, and they protect people who've been humiliated."},"order":["Hanun's father dies","David sends men to comfort Hanun","Advisers say they are spies","Hanun shaves half their beards","David tells them to wait in Jericho","Joab and Abishai win the war"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did David send men to Hanun?","o":["To comfort him after his father died","To spy","To attack","To trade"],"why":"2 Samuel 10:2."},{"q":"What did Hanun do to David's men?","o":["Shaved half their beards and cut their robes","Gave them gifts","Made them guards","Sent them to Egypt"],"why":"2 Samuel 10:4."},{"q":"Where did David tell them to wait?","o":["Jericho, until their beards grew back","Jerusalem","Egypt","The desert"],"why":"2 Samuel 10:5."}],"words":["BEARDS","HANUN","KINDNESS","SPIES","ASHAMED","JERICHO"]},"s2g":{"passage":[[11,1,5],[11,14,17],[11,26,27]],"intro":[{"sc":"rooftop","n":"In the spring, when kings usually go out to battle, David stayed home in Jerusalem. One evening he walked on the palace roof, and saw a very beautiful woman bathing."},{"sc":"rooftop","n":"She was Bathsheba, the wife of Uriah, one of David's most loyal soldiers, who was away fighting. David was the king, and he took what he wanted. He sent for her and slept with her, and she became pregnant."},{"sc":"rooftop","n":"To hide what he had done, David tried to trick Uriah. But Uriah was too honourable to go home and rest while his fellow soldiers were in tents at war."},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"So David did something even worse. He wrote a letter to Joab, and made Uriah carry it himself: \"Put Uriah at the front of the fiercest fighting, then pull back so he dies.\" And Uriah was killed."},{"sc":"rooftop","n":"David married Bathsheba. Everyone thought it was over. But the Bible says: \"The thing David had done displeased the Lord.\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Small steps into big sin","text":"It started with being in the wrong place (home instead of at work), then a look, then a choice, then a lie, then a murder. One sin led to another to cover it up. Even the greatest leaders can fall. Guard your eyes and your heart early, and never try to cover up wrong with more wrong."},"order":["David stays home from battle","He sees Bathsheba from the roof","David takes Bathsheba","David tries to trick Uriah","Uriah is sent to the front line and dies","God is displeased with David"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where should David have been?","o":["Out at battle with his army","In Hebron","In the temple","Asleep"],"why":"2 Samuel 11:1."},{"q":"Who was Uriah?","o":["Bathsheba's husband, a loyal soldier","David's son","A prophet","A Philistine"],"why":"2 Samuel 11:3."},{"q":"How did David try to cover up his sin?","o":["With lies, and then by having Uriah killed","By confessing","By running away","By giving gold"],"why":"One sin led to another."}],"words":["ROOFTOP","URIAH","LETTER","SIN","COVERUP","BATHSHEBA"]},"s2h":{"passage":[[12,1,14],[12,24,25]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"God sent the prophet Nathan to David. Nathan didn't start by accusing him. He told a story."},{"sc":"lamb","w":"bnathan","e":"worried","t":"A rich man had huge flocks of sheep. A poor man had just one little lamb. It grew up with his children, ate from his plate and slept in his arms, like a daughter."},{"sc":"lamb","w":"bnathan","e":"frown","t":"A traveller came to visit the rich man. Instead of using one of his own sheep, he took the poor man's lamb and cooked it for his guest."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"frown","t":"As the Lord lives, the man who did this deserves to die!"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bnathan","e":"stern","t":"You are that man!"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"I have sinned against the Lord."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Nathan said, \"The Lord has taken away your sin. You will not die.\" But there would still be painful consequences in David's family. David poured out his heart in Psalm 51: \"Create in me a clean heart, O God.\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Truth-tellers and true repentance","text":"Nathan was brave enough to confront a king, and wise enough to use a story so David would see it clearly. David didn't make excuses like Saul did. He said, 'I have sinned.' Leaders need people who tell them the truth, and they repent honestly when they're wrong."},"order":["God sends Nathan to David","Nathan tells the story of the little lamb","David is furious with the rich man","Nathan says, \"You are that man!\"","David says, \"I have sinned\"","God forgives David"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did the poor man in the story have?","o":["One little lamb","A big flock","A camel","A field"],"why":"2 Samuel 12:3."},{"q":"What did Nathan say to David?","o":["\"You are that man!\"","\"Well done\"","\"Go to war\"","\"Build a temple\""],"why":"2 Samuel 12:7."},{"q":"What did David say?","o":["\"I have sinned against the Lord\"","\"It wasn't me\"","\"Get out!\"","Nothing"],"why":"2 Samuel 12:13."}],"words":["NATHAN","LAMB","STORY","SINNED","FORGIVEN","CLEAN"]},"s2h2":{"passage":[[12,15,25],[12,26,31]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"As Nathan had warned, the baby born to David and Bathsheba became very sick. David refused to eat. He lay on the ground all night, praying for the child."},{"sc":"throne","n":"On the seventh day, the baby died. The servants were afraid to tell David. But when he saw them whispering, he knew."},{"sc":"throne","n":"David got up, washed, changed his clothes, worshipped God, and ate. His servants were confused. \"While the child was alive, I fasted and wept, hoping God might be gracious. Now he's gone. I'll go to him one day, but he won't come back to me.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"David comforted Bathsheba. Later they had another son: Solomon. God sent Nathan with a special name for him: Jedidiah, which means 'loved by the Lord'."},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"Joab captured the Ammonite city of Rabbah, and David took the king's heavy golden crown, set with a precious stone, and it was placed on his head."}],"lesson":{"title":"Grief, hope and new beginnings","text":"David prayed hard, accepted God's answer, and found hope: he would see his child again one day. Then God gave a new son and a beautiful name, 'loved by the Lord'. Grief is real, but God brings new beginnings after sorrow."},"order":["The baby becomes sick","David fasts and prays for seven days","The baby dies","David worships and eats","Solomon is born","God names him Jedidiah"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did David do while the baby was sick?","o":["Fasted and prayed","Went to war","Held a feast","Nothing"],"why":"2 Samuel 12:16."},{"q":"What does Jedidiah mean?","o":["Loved by the Lord","Wise king","Peace","Strong"],"why":"2 Samuel 12:25."},{"q":"What was Solomon's other special name?","o":["Jedidiah","Ichabod","Israel","Ebenezer"],"why":"2 Samuel 12:25."}],"words":["SOLOMON","JEDIDIAH","GRIEF","HOPE","PRAYER","CROWN"]},"s2i":{"passage":[[13,15,22],[13,28,29],[13,37,39]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Just as Nathan warned, trouble came into David's own family. David's oldest son, Amnon, became obsessed with his half-sister Tamar."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Amnon tricked her into coming to his room by pretending to be sick, and then he forced himself on her and hurt her terribly. Afterwards he threw her out and locked the door."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Tamar tore her beautiful princess robe, put ashes on her head and went away crying out loud. Her brother Absalom took her into his home, where she lived, heartbroken."},{"sc":"throne","n":"King David was very angry, but he did nothing to Amnon. Absalom said nothing either, but he hated Amnon."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Two years later, at a feast, Absalom had his servants kill Amnon. Then Absalom ran away to another country for three years."}],"lesson":{"title":"Speak up and act on wrong","text":"What happened to Tamar was a terrible wrong, and it was not her fault. David was angry, but he didn't act, and his silence let bitterness grow until it exploded. If someone ever hurts you or forces you to do something, tell a trusted adult. And leaders must act justly when wrong is done, not stay silent."},"order":["Amnon becomes obsessed with Tamar","He tricks and hurts her","Tamar tears her robe and weeps","Absalom takes Tamar in","David is angry but does nothing","Absalom takes revenge and runs away"],"quiz":[{"q":"Was what happened to Tamar her fault?","o":["No, it was a terrible wrong done to her","Yes","Partly","Nobody knows"],"why":"She was a victim."},{"q":"What did David do about Amnon?","o":["He was angry but did nothing","He punished him","He banished him","He made him king"],"why":"2 Samuel 13:21."},{"q":"What should you do if someone hurts you like this?","o":["Tell a trusted adult","Keep it a secret","Blame yourself","Take revenge"],"why":"You are never alone."}],"words":["TAMAR","WRONG","SILENCE","JUSTICE","TRUSTED","ADULT"]},"s2i2":{"passage":[[14,1,14],[14,21,24],[14,28,33]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Absalom had been in exile for three years. Joab could see that David missed his son. So he made a plan, with a wise woman from Tekoa."},{"sc":"throne","w":"btekoa","e":"worried","t":"Help me, O king! I'm a widow. My two sons fought, and one killed the other. Now my family wants to kill my last son. Then I'll have no one left!"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"As the Lord lives, not one hair of your son's head will fall to the ground."},{"sc":"throne","w":"btekoa","e":"stern","t":"Then why won't the king bring home his OWN banished son? We all must die, like water spilled on the ground. But God makes ways for the banished not to stay cut off."},{"sc":"foxfire","n":"David let Absalom come home, but wouldn't see him for two years. Absalom sent for Joab twice, and Joab ignored him. So Absalom ordered his servants to set Joab's barley field on fire! That got Joab's attention."}],"lesson":{"title":"Make a way back","text":"The woman's story helped David see that he should make a way home for his son, just as God makes ways for the banished to return. But half-hearted reconciliation, coming home without really being welcomed, left Absalom bitter. Leaders make full, real peace."},"order":["Absalom is in exile for three years","Joab plans with the wise woman","She tells the king a story","David promises to protect her son","She asks why he won't bring his own son home","Absalom sets Joab's field on fire"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where was the wise woman from?","o":["Tekoa","Abel","Endor","Jericho"],"why":"2 Samuel 14:2."},{"q":"Who planned her visit to the king?","o":["Joab","Absalom","Nathan","Bathsheba"],"why":"2 Samuel 14:3."},{"q":"How did Absalom get Joab's attention?","o":["Set his barley field on fire","Wrote a letter","Shouted at the gate","Sent a gift"],"why":"2 Samuel 14:30."}],"words":["TEKOA","WISE","EXILE","HOME","BARLEY","FIRE"]},"s2j":{"passage":[[14,25,26],[15,1,14]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"Absalom came back to Jerusalem. He was the most handsome man in Israel, with no flaw from head to toe. His hair was so thick that when he cut it once a year, it weighed over two kilos!"},{"sc":"citygate","n":"He got himself a chariot, horses and fifty men to run ahead of him. Every morning he stood by the city gate."},{"sc":"citygate","w":"babsalom","e":"smile","t":"Ah, your case is good and right. But the king has nobody to listen to you. If only I were judge, I'd give everyone justice!"},{"sc":"citygate","n":"When people bowed to him, he pulled them up and kissed them. For four years he did this, and the Bible says he stole the hearts of the people of Israel."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"Then Absalom declared himself king in Hebron! A messenger ran to David: \"The hearts of the people are with Absalom!\" David said, \"We must flee, or none of us will escape!\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Beware of flattery","text":"Absalom didn't win people by serving them. He won them with charm, flattery and promises he never meant to keep. Leaders lead by real service, and they're wise about people who say exactly what you want to hear."},"order":["Absalom returns to Jerusalem","He gets chariots and runners","He waits at the city gate","He flatters everyone with a complaint","He steals the people's hearts","Absalom declares himself king"],"quiz":[{"q":"What was Absalom famous for?","o":["His good looks and thick hair","His singing","His wisdom","His kindness"],"why":"2 Samuel 14:25-26."},{"q":"Where did Absalom wait each morning?","o":["By the city gate","At the temple","In the field","On the roof"],"why":"2 Samuel 15:2."},{"q":"What did Absalom steal?","o":["The hearts of the people","The ark","Gold","Horses"],"why":"2 Samuel 15:6."}],"words":["ABSALOM","HAIR","FLATTERY","GATE","HEARTS","CHARM"]},"s2j2":{"passage":[[15,13,29]],"intro":[{"sc":"wilderness","n":"As David fled Jerusalem from Absalom, his loyal soldiers marched past him, including 600 men from the Philistine city of Gath, led by Ittai."},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"Why are you coming with us, Ittai? You're a foreigner. You only arrived yesterday! Go back and stay with the new king."},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bittai","e":"stern","t":"As surely as the Lord lives, and as my lord the king lives, wherever my lord the king is, whether in life or in death, there your servant will be!"},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"David let him come. Then the priests Zadok and Abiathar arrived carrying the ark of God, to go with David."},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"Take the ark back into the city. If God is pleased with me, He'll bring me back. If not, let Him do what seems good to Him. And send your sons Ahimaaz and Jonathan to me with news."}],"lesson":{"title":"Loyalty in the hard times","text":"A foreigner who'd only just arrived stayed loyal when many of David's own people abandoned him. And David refused to use the ark as a lucky charm, trusting God's will instead. Leaders value loyal friends, and trust God rather than religious objects."},"order":["David flees from Absalom","Ittai and 600 men march past","David tells Ittai to go back","Ittai promises loyalty in life or death","The priests bring the ark","David sends the ark back to Jerusalem"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where was Ittai from?","o":["Gath, a Philistine city","Jerusalem","Bethlehem","Egypt"],"why":"2 Samuel 15:19."},{"q":"What did Ittai promise?","o":["To be with David in life or death","To go home","To fight Absalom alone","To become king"],"why":"2 Samuel 15:21."},{"q":"What did David do with the ark?","o":["Sent it back to Jerusalem","Took it with him","Gave it to Absalom","Hid it"],"why":"2 Samuel 15:25."}],"words":["ITTAI","LOYAL","GATH","ZADOK","ARK","FOREIGNER"]},"s2k":{"passage":[[15,30,37],[16,5,14]],"intro":[{"sc":"wilderness","n":"King David left Jerusalem on foot, barefoot and weeping, with his head covered, up the Mount of Olives. All his loyal people wept with him."},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"His wise friend Hushai came to join him, but David said, \"Go back. Pretend to serve Absalom, and spoil the advice of his clever adviser Ahithophel.\""},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"Along the road, a man from Saul's family named Shimei came out. He threw stones and dirt at David and shouted curses."},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bshimei","e":"frown","t":"Get out, get out, you man of blood, you worthless man! The Lord is paying you back!"},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bjoab","e":"frown","t":"Why should this dead dog curse my lord the king? Let me go over and take off his head!"},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"Leave him alone. Maybe the Lord will see my trouble, and repay me with good for his cursing today."}],"lesson":{"title":"Humility in the hardest moment","text":"David lost his palace, his city and his son's love, and then was cursed in the street. He could have lashed out. Instead, he stayed humble and trusted God with his reputation. Leaders keep their dignity and humility even when everything goes wrong."},"order":["David leaves Jerusalem weeping","Hushai comes to join David","David sends Hushai back as a secret friend","Shimei throws stones and curses","Abishai wants to strike Shimei","David says, \"Leave him alone\""],"quiz":[{"q":"How did David leave Jerusalem?","o":["Barefoot and weeping","In a chariot","With a big army","Secretly at night"],"why":"2 Samuel 15:30."},{"q":"What did Shimei do?","o":["Threw stones and cursed David","Gave David food","Joined Absalom","Bowed down"],"why":"2 Samuel 16:6."},{"q":"What did David say about Shimei?","o":["Leave him alone","Kill him","Arrest him","Ignore the stones"],"why":"2 Samuel 16:11."}],"words":["SHIMEI","HUSHAI","OLIVES","CURSE","HUMBLE","FLEE"]},"s2k2":{"passage":[[16,1,4],[16,15,23],[17,1,14]],"intro":[{"sc":"wilderness","n":"As David fled, Ziba, Mephibosheth's servant, brought donkeys loaded with food. \"Where's your master?\" David asked. Ziba lied: \"He stayed in Jerusalem, hoping to get Saul's kingdom back!\" David believed him."},{"sc":"throne","n":"In Jerusalem, Absalom had the most famous adviser in Israel: Ahithophel. People said his advice was like hearing the word of God. Ahithophel told Absalom to do something shameful on the palace roof, to show everyone he had taken his father's place."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bahithophel","e":"stern","t":"Let me choose 12,000 men and attack David tonight, while he's tired. Everyone will run. I'll strike down only the king."},{"sc":"throne","n":"It was a brilliant plan. But Absalom said, \"Let's also hear what Hushai says.\""},{"sc":"throne","w":"bhushai","e":"stern","t":"This time Ahithophel's advice isn't good. Your father is a fierce warrior, angry as a bear robbed of her cubs. Gather ALL Israel first, as many as the sand on the seashore, and lead them yourself!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"Absalom chose Hushai's advice. The Bible explains: the Lord had decided to defeat Ahithophel's good advice. It gave David the time he needed to escape."}],"lesson":{"title":"God is behind the scenes","text":"Ahithophel was brilliant, but he used his gifts for a rebellion. Hushai, David's loyal friend, risked his life working undercover. And God was at work behind it all. Being clever isn't enough. Leaders use their gifts for what's right, and trust God's hidden hand."},"order":["Ziba lies about Mephibosheth","Ahithophel advises Absalom","Ahithophel plans to attack David tonight","Absalom asks Hushai too","Hushai says gather all Israel first","Absalom chooses Hushai's advice"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did people say about Ahithophel's advice?","o":["It was like hearing the word of God","It was foolish","It was slow","Nobody listened"],"why":"2 Samuel 16:23."},{"q":"What did Hushai compare David to?","o":["A bear robbed of her cubs","A sleeping lion","A frightened rabbit","A tired donkey"],"why":"2 Samuel 17:8."},{"q":"Why was Hushai's advice chosen?","o":["The Lord had decided to defeat Ahithophel's plan","It was shorter","Absalom liked Hushai more","By accident"],"why":"2 Samuel 17:14."}],"words":["HUSHAI","AHITHOPHEL","ADVICE","ZIBA","BEAR","SECRET"]},"s2k3":{"passage":[[17,15,29]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"Hushai rushed the secret plans to the priests Zadok and Abiathar. Their sons, Jonathan and Ahimaaz, were waiting at a spring outside the city, ready to run with messages to David."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"A servant girl brought them the message. But a boy saw them and told Absalom! They ran to a house in the village of Bahurim."},{"sc":"well","n":"The house had a well in the courtyard. The two young men climbed down into it. The woman of the house spread a cloth over the top and scattered grain on it, so it looked like grain drying in the sun."},{"sc":"well","w":"bpeople","e":"frown","t":"Where are Ahimaaz and Jonathan?"},{"sc":"well","n":"\"They've gone across the stream!\" the woman said. Absalom's men searched, found nothing, and went back. The two climbed out and ran all the way to David: \"Cross the river tonight!\""},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"David's people crossed the Jordan by morning. At Mahanaim, three generous men, Shobi, Machir and old Barzillai, brought beds, bowls, wheat, barley, beans, honey, cheese and sheep for the hungry, tired, thirsty people."}],"lesson":{"title":"Brave messengers and hidden helpers","text":"Two young men risked their lives carrying the truth. An unnamed woman hid them in a well. Generous strangers fed an exhausted army. Big victories often depend on brave, ordinary people doing their part quietly."},"order":["Hushai sends the secret plans","Ahimaaz and Jonathan wait at a spring","A boy reports them to Absalom","They hide in a well","A woman covers the well with grain","They warn David to cross the river"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where did Ahimaaz and Jonathan hide?","o":["In a well","In a cave","Up a tree","In a cart"],"why":"2 Samuel 17:18."},{"q":"How did the woman hide the well?","o":["Spread a cloth with grain on it","Put a rock on it","Filled it with water","Sat on it"],"why":"2 Samuel 17:19."},{"q":"What did Barzillai and the others bring David's people?","o":["Beds, bowls, food, honey and cheese","Swords","Horses","Gold"],"why":"2 Samuel 17:28-29."}],"words":["AHIMAAZ","WELL","GRAIN","BAHURIM","MESSAGE","HONEY"]},"s2l":{"passage":[[18,1,15],[18,31,33]],"intro":[{"sc":"battlehill","n":"The battle came in the forest of Ephraim. Before it began, David gave all his commanders one order, in front of everyone: \"Be gentle with the young man Absalom, for my sake.\""},{"sc":"oak","n":"David's army won. Absalom fled on his mule, and rode under the thick branches of a great oak tree. His long, thick hair got caught in the branches!"},{"sc":"oak","n":"The mule ran on, and Absalom was left hanging in the air between heaven and earth."},{"sc":"oak","w":"bjoab","e":"frown","t":"You saw him and didn't kill him? I won't waste time with you!"},{"sc":"oak","n":"Joab ignored the king's order, and Absalom was killed."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"O my son Absalom! My son, my son Absalom! If only I had died instead of you! O Absalom, my son, my son!"}],"lesson":{"title":"A father's love never quits","text":"Absalom had betrayed David, stolen his throne and tried to kill him. Yet David still loved him, and wept for him. That's a picture of God's love for rebellious children. Leaders keep loving, even those who have hurt them."},"order":["David says, \"Be gentle with Absalom\"","David's army wins","Absalom's hair catches in an oak tree","Joab ignores the king's order","Absalom dies","David weeps, \"O my son Absalom!\""],"quiz":[{"q":"What got caught in the tree?","o":["Absalom's hair","His sword","His crown","His robe"],"why":"2 Samuel 18:9."},{"q":"What order did David give about Absalom?","o":["Be gentle with him","Kill him","Bring his crown","Banish him"],"why":"2 Samuel 18:5."},{"q":"How did David react to Absalom's death?","o":["He wept bitterly","He celebrated","He was angry at Absalom","He didn't care"],"why":"2 Samuel 18:33."}],"words":["ABSALOM","OAK","HAIR","MULE","WEEP","FATHER"]},"s2l2":{"passage":[[18,19,32]],"intro":[{"sc":"runners","n":"After the battle, Ahimaaz begged Joab: \"Let me run and tell the king the good news!\" But Joab knew the news about Absalom would break David's heart."},{"sc":"runners","n":"Joab sent a Cushite instead. But Ahimaaz kept begging: \"Whatever happens, please let me run too!\" Finally Joab said, \"Run.\""},{"sc":"runners","n":"Ahimaaz took the road through the plain, and he overtook the Cushite!"},{"sc":"citygate","n":"David sat between the two gates. The watchman on the roof called, \"I see a man running alone! And another! The first one runs like Ahimaaz!\" David said, \"He's a good man. He brings good news.\""},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bahimaaz","e":"big","t":"All is well! Blessed be the Lord, who has delivered the men who rebelled against my lord the king!"},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"Is the young man Absalom safe?"},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bahimaaz","e":"worried","t":"I saw a great commotion... but I don't know what it was."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"Ahimaaz couldn't bring himself to tell the sad part. Then the Cushite arrived and told the king the truth about Absalom."}],"lesson":{"title":"Messengers carry hearts too","text":"Ahimaaz loved to bring good news, and he was so fast! But when it came to the painful part, he froze. Telling hard truths kindly is a real skill. Leaders learn to share both good news and bad news honestly and gently."},"order":["Ahimaaz begs to run with the news","Joab sends the Cushite instead","Ahimaaz begs again and runs","He overtakes the Cushite","He tells David the victory","He can't tell the sad news"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who did Joab send first?","o":["A Cushite","Ahimaaz","Abishai","Hushai"],"why":"2 Samuel 18:21."},{"q":"How did Ahimaaz overtake the Cushite?","o":["He ran by the road through the plain","He rode a horse","He took a shortcut through the forest","He swam"],"why":"2 Samuel 18:23."},{"q":"What did Ahimaaz say about Absalom?","o":["He saw a commotion but didn't know what it was","Absalom was safe","Absalom was king","Nothing"],"why":"2 Samuel 18:29."}],"words":["AHIMAAZ","RUNNER","CUSHITE","NEWS","WATCHMAN","GATES"]},"s2l3":{"passage":[[19,1,8]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"David's army had won and saved his life, but the king covered his face and kept crying, \"O my son Absalom!\""},{"sc":"citygate","n":"The soldiers crept back into the city quietly, as if they had lost the battle and were ashamed."},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bjoab","e":"frown","t":"Today you have humiliated all the men who saved your life and your family's! You love those who hate you, and hate those who love you!"},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bjoab","e":"stern","t":"Now get up! Go out and speak kindly to your men. If you don't, not one of them will stay with you tonight."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"David got up and sat in the gate. When the people heard, they all came to their king."}],"lesson":{"title":"Grieve, but don't forget your people","text":"David's grief was real and right. But his soldiers had risked everything for him, and felt ignored. Joab's hard words were blunt, but true. Leaders allow themselves to grieve, and they still show up for the people who depend on them."},"order":["David weeps for Absalom","The soldiers creep in ashamed","Joab rebukes the king","Joab tells David to encourage his men","David sits in the gate","The people come to their king"],"quiz":[{"q":"How did the soldiers come back into the city?","o":["Quietly, as if ashamed","Cheering loudly","Singing","Riding horses"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:3."},{"q":"What did Joab tell David to do?","o":["Go out and speak kindly to his men","Leave the city","Punish the soldiers","Hold a feast"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:7."},{"q":"Where did David go to sit?","o":["In the gate","In his room","On the roof","In the temple"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:8."}],"words":["JOAB","GRIEF","SOLDIERS","GATE","KINDLY","HONEST"]},"s2l4":{"passage":[[19,15,30]],"intro":[{"sc":"jordan","n":"As David came back to cross the Jordan, guess who ran to meet him first? Shimei, the man who had thrown stones and cursed him!"},{"sc":"jordan","w":"bshimei","e":"worried","t":"My lord, please don't hold my wrong against me! I know I sinned. That's why I'm the first one here today!"},{"sc":"jordan","w":"babishai","e":"frown","t":"Shouldn't Shimei die for cursing the Lord's anointed king?"},{"sc":"jordan","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"Today is not a day for anyone to die. Shimei, you will not die."},{"sc":"jordan","n":"Then Mephibosheth came. He hadn't washed his feet, trimmed his moustache or washed his clothes since the day David left, because he had been mourning for the king."},{"sc":"jordan","w":"bmephib","e":"worried","t":"My servant Ziba tricked me and lied about me! I'm lame, so I couldn't come. But my lord is like an angel of God. Do whatever you think is right."}],"lesson":{"title":"Forgive, and look again","text":"David forgave Shimei on a day of victory, when he could easily have taken revenge. And he discovered that the story he'd believed about Mephibosheth was a lie. Leaders forgive generously, and they're willing to change their minds when they learn the truth."},"order":["David returns to cross the Jordan","Shimei runs to meet him first","Shimei begs forgiveness","David spares Shimei","Mephibosheth comes, unwashed","Mephibosheth explains Ziba's lie"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who ran to meet David first?","o":["Shimei, who had cursed him","Joab","Ahimaaz","Nathan"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:16."},{"q":"Why hadn't Mephibosheth washed his feet?","o":["He had been mourning since David left","He had no water","He forgot","He was lazy"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:24."},{"q":"Who had lied about Mephibosheth?","o":["His servant Ziba","Shimei","Joab","Absalom"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:26."}],"words":["SHIMEI","MERCY","FORGIVE","ZIBA","TRUTH","JORDAN"]},"s2m":{"passage":[[19,31,39],[20,14,22]],"intro":[{"sc":"wilderness","n":"As David returned to Jerusalem, an 80-year-old man named Barzillai came to say goodbye. He had fed David's whole army in the wilderness. David invited him to live at the palace, but Barzillai said, \"I'm too old. Take my son instead.\""},{"sc":"citygate","n":"Then another rebel named Sheba rose up. Joab's army chased him to the town of Abel and began battering down its walls."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"On the way, Joab met Amasa, a commander David had appointed in his place. Joab greeted him like a friend, \"Are you well, my brother?\", took his beard to kiss him... and with the sword in his other hand, struck him down. Ruthless Joab had removed another rival."},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bwisewoman","e":"stern","t":"Listen! Listen! Tell Joab to come here, so I can speak with him."},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bwisewoman","e":"stern","t":"We're a peaceful, faithful town in Israel. Why are you trying to destroy it?"},{"sc":"citygate","w":"bjoab","e":"stern","t":"We don't want to destroy your town! We just want one rebel: Sheba. Give him up, and we'll leave."},{"sc":"citygate","n":"The wise woman went to her people with her wise advice. The rebel was handed over, Joab blew the trumpet, and the whole town was saved, by one woman's courage and wisdom."}],"lesson":{"title":"One wise voice can save a city","text":"An old man's generosity fed a king's army. An unnamed woman stood on a wall and spoke up while soldiers were battering down the gates. She saved a whole town. Leaders aren't always famous. Sometimes they're the one wise voice in a crisis."},"order":["Barzillai says goodbye to David","Sheba leads a rebellion","Joab attacks the town of Abel","A wise woman calls from the wall","She talks with Joab","The town is saved"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who had fed David's army in the wilderness?","o":["Barzillai","Joab","Shimei","Nathan"],"why":"2 Samuel 19:32."},{"q":"Who called out from the town wall?","o":["A wise woman","A soldier","A priest","A child"],"why":"2 Samuel 20:16."},{"q":"How was the town saved?","o":["The wise woman's advice handed over the rebel, and Joab left","A battle","A miracle storm","They paid gold"],"why":"2 Samuel 20:22."}],"words":["WISDOM","WALL","ABEL","BARZILLAI","SHEBA","SAVED"]},"s2n":{"passage":[[21,1,3],[21,8,14],[21,15,22]],"intro":[{"sc":"rizpah","n":"There was a famine for three years. God said it was because Saul had broken Israel's ancient peace promise with the Gibeonites, and had tried to wipe them out."},{"sc":"rizpah","n":"The Gibeonites demanded justice, and seven of Saul's descendants were put to death. Two of them were the sons of a woman named Rizpah."},{"sc":"rizpah","n":"Rizpah spread rough cloth on a rock beside their bodies. From the beginning of the harvest until the rains came, months later, she kept watch. By day she chased away the birds; by night, the wild animals."},{"sc":"rizpah","n":"When David heard what she had done, he was moved. He gathered the bones of Saul and Jonathan too, and buried them all properly in the family tomb. Then God answered prayer for the land."},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"Later, there were more battles with giants from Gath. David's men defeated them, including one huge man who had six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot: 24 in all!"}],"lesson":{"title":"Faithful love moves hearts","text":"Rizpah had no power and no voice in the palace. But her faithful, lonely watch spoke louder than any speech, and moved the king to do what was right. Never underestimate quiet faithfulness."},"order":["Famine comes for three years","Saul's broken promise is revealed","Rizpah's sons are put to death","Rizpah keeps watch for months","David buries Saul's family properly","David's men defeat the giants"],"quiz":[{"q":"How long did Rizpah keep watch?","o":["From harvest until the rains came","One night","Forty days","One week"],"why":"2 Samuel 21:10."},{"q":"What did Rizpah chase away?","o":["Birds by day and wild animals by night","Soldiers","Thieves","Nobody"],"why":"2 Samuel 21:10."},{"q":"How many fingers and toes did the giant have?","o":["24","20","10","30"],"why":"2 Samuel 21:20."}],"words":["RIZPAH","WATCH","FAMINE","GIANTS","FINGERS","FAITHFUL"]},"s2n2":{"passage":[[22,1,20],[23,1,7]],"intro":[{"sc":"cliffs","n":"Near the end of his life, David sang a great song to God, for rescuing him from all his enemies and from Saul."},{"sc":"cliffs","w":"bkingdavid","e":"big","t":"The Lord is my rock, my fortress and my rescuer! My God is my rock, where I hide. My shield, my strong tower!"},{"sc":"cliffs","w":"bkingdavid","e":"big","t":"With You I can run against an army. With my God I can leap over a wall!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"And these were David's last words, the words of 'the sweet singer of Israel':"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"smile","t":"When someone rules over people with justice, ruling in the fear of God, he is like the morning light at sunrise, like the sunshine after rain that makes the grass grow."}],"lesson":{"title":"Look back with thanks","text":"At the end of a long, hard life, David didn't brag about his victories. He gave all the credit to God, his rock and rescuer. And his final advice was about good leadership: a just leader is like sunshine after rain. Leaders end with gratitude."},"order":["David looks back on his life","He sings to God, his rock and fortress","He sings, \"With my God I can leap a wall\"","David speaks his last words","A just ruler is like morning light"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did David call God in his song?","o":["My rock, my fortress, my rescuer","My king only","My friend only","My servant"],"why":"2 Samuel 22:2."},{"q":"\"With my God I can leap over a...\"","o":["Wall","River","Mountain","Giant"],"why":"2 Samuel 22:30."},{"q":"What is a just ruler like, according to David's last words?","o":["Morning light and sunshine after rain","A storm","A sword","A tall tower"],"why":"2 Samuel 23:4."}],"words":["ROCK","FORTRESS","SHIELD","SONG","SUNRISE","JUSTICE"]},"s2o2":{"passage":[[23,8,17]],"intro":[{"sc":"battlehill","n":"David's mightiest warriors were known as 'The Three'. First was Josheb-basshebeth, the chief, who fought 800 enemies at one time!"},{"sc":"battlehill","w":"beleazar","e":"stern","t":"The others ran. I'm staying!"},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"That was Eleazar. When Israel's army retreated, he stood and fought the Philistines until his hand was so tired that it froze to his sword! The Lord gave a great victory, and the army came back only to collect the spoil."},{"sc":"lentilfield","n":"Then Shammah. The Philistines gathered at a field full of lentils, and Israel's troops ran. Shammah stood right in the middle of that field, defended it, and struck down the Philistines."},{"sc":"well","n":"Once, when David was hiding in a cave, he sighed, \"If only someone would bring me a drink from the well by the gate of Bethlehem!\" The Three broke through the Philistine camp, drew water from the well, and carried it back to him."},{"sc":"well","n":"But David wouldn't drink it. He poured it out to the Lord: \"Far be it from me! This is the blood of men who risked their lives!\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Stand your ground","text":"Eleazar and Shammah stood firm when everyone around them ran, for a battlefield and a bean field! Sometimes courage means staying when others leave. And David honoured his men's sacrifice instead of taking it for granted."},"order":["Josheb-basshebeth fights 800","Eleazar's hand freezes to his sword","Shammah defends the lentil field","David longs for water from Bethlehem","The Three break through and fetch it","David pours it out to the Lord"],"quiz":[{"q":"What happened to Eleazar's hand?","o":["It froze to his sword","It was broken","It turned gold","He lost it"],"why":"2 Samuel 23:10."},{"q":"What field did Shammah defend?","o":["A field of lentils","A wheat field","A vineyard","A sheep pen"],"why":"2 Samuel 23:11."},{"q":"Why did David pour the water out?","o":["His men had risked their lives for it","It was dirty","He wasn't thirsty","It was poisoned"],"why":"2 Samuel 23:17."}],"words":["ELEAZAR","SHAMMAH","LENTILS","THREE","WELL","STAND"]},"s2o3":{"passage":[[21,15,17],[23,18,39]],"intro":[{"sc":"battlehill","n":"Once, when David was old and grew tired in battle, a giant named Ishbi-benob, with a huge bronze spear, decided to kill him. Abishai rushed in and saved David's life!"},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"After that, David's men said, \"You must never go out to battle with us again! You are the lamp of Israel. We can't let that light go out.\""},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"Abishai was chief of the next group of heroes, 'the Thirty'. He once fought 300 men with his spear."},{"sc":"snowpit","n":"Benaiah defeated two of Moab's mightiest warriors. He went down into a pit on a snowy day and killed a lion. He fought a huge Egyptian armed only with a club, snatched the spear out of his hand and won. David put him in charge of his bodyguard."},{"sc":"battlehill","n":"The Thirty included swift Asahel, Elhanan of Bethlehem, and many others. And the very last name on the list is a sad one: Uriah the Hittite. A reminder that heroes are real people, with real stories."}],"lesson":{"title":"Protect your leaders, and be remembered","text":"David's men loved him so much they risked their lives for him, and protected him when he was weak. The Bible remembers each of their names. Leaders build loyal teams, and loyal teams protect their leaders. Your faithfulness matters, and God remembers names."},"order":["Abishai saves David from the giant Ishbi-benob","David's men call him the lamp of Israel","Abishai fights 300 men","Benaiah fights a lion in a snowy pit","Benaiah defeats a huge Egyptian","The list of the Thirty ends with Uriah"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who saved David from the giant Ishbi-benob?","o":["Abishai","Benaiah","Joab","Asahel"],"why":"2 Samuel 21:17."},{"q":"What did David's men call him?","o":["The lamp of Israel","The lion of Judah","The giant-slayer","The shepherd"],"why":"2 Samuel 21:17."},{"q":"Whose name is last on the list of the Thirty?","o":["Uriah the Hittite","Benaiah","Abishai","Joab"],"why":"2 Samuel 23:39."}],"words":["ABISHAI","BENAIAH","THIRTY","LAMP","SNOW","URIAH"]},"s2p":{"passage":[[24,1,4],[24,10,25]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"Near the end of his life, David ordered a count of all his fighting men. Joab warned him not to. David wanted to feel proud of his army's size, instead of trusting God."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bkingdavid","e":"worried","t":"I have sinned greatly in what I have done. Please, Lord, take away my guilt. I have acted very foolishly."},{"sc":"threshing","n":"A terrible plague came, and spread towards Jerusalem. The angel of the Lord stopped at the threshing floor of a man named Araunah."},{"sc":"threshing","n":"God told David to build an altar there. Araunah offered to give David the land, the oxen and the wood for free."},{"sc":"threshing","w":"bkingdavid","e":"stern","t":"No. I will pay you for it. I will not offer to the Lord my God anything that costs me nothing."},{"sc":"threshing","n":"David bought the threshing floor and offered sacrifices, and the plague stopped. Years later, his son Solomon built God's temple on that very spot."}],"lesson":{"title":"Real worship costs something","text":"David could have given God a free offering. He refused, because giving something that costs nothing isn't really giving. Leaders give God their best, not their leftovers, and they take responsibility for their mistakes."},"order":["David orders a count of his army","Joab warns him not to","David admits his sin","A plague comes","Araunah offers his land for free","David insists on paying"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did David count?","o":["His fighting men","His gold","His sheep","His sons"],"why":"2 Samuel 24:2."},{"q":"Whose threshing floor did David buy?","o":["Araunah's","Boaz's","Joab's","Nathan's"],"why":"2 Samuel 24:24."},{"q":"What was later built on that spot?","o":["Solomon's temple","A palace","A market","A tower"],"why":"2 Chronicles 3:1."}],"words":["ARAUNAH","ALTAR","COST","PLAGUE","OFFERING","TEMPLE"]}});
